      "content": "The dialogue is still feeling a bit too coded and \"safe.\" Even for a prestige drama, people in that era wouldn't always speak in metaphors about \"rivers\" or \"currents\" when they were angry or scared about their social standing. They were often much more blunt, even derogatory, about the people they saw as a threat to their way of life.\n\nTo really make this feel like a real historical piece, I need the dialogue to reflect the actual prejudice of the time. Can you push the scene further? Have them express their actual fears—the idea that their \"kind of people\" are being pushed out, or use the kind of language that actually defined the Jim Crow era? I'm looking for that raw, unfiltered bitterness where they aren't just worried about business, but are expressing a deep-seated belief in the racial hierarchy. Make it uncomfortable to read, because that's how it was.",
